 :what-the-seventh-key-cost
 "BELOW THE BENCHMARK'S NOISE FLOOR. Three alternating JVMs per engine,
  min-of-six within each: THE SPREAD OF ONE ENGINE AGAINST ITSELF on one
  stylesheet (54.6-67.2 ms) IS WIDER THAN ANY GAP BETWEEN THE TWO, and the
  distributions overlap. Structurally it is one :proximity lookup per
  declaration per comparison, one :rule/scope lookup per rule per element,
  and one unforced `delay` per element -- every unscoped declaration
  carries the same ##-Inf, so THE KEY IS A PURE TIE, WHICH IS WHY THE OPS
  DUMP IS BYTE-IDENTICAL. A second comparator for the no-@scope case was
  considered and NOT written: provably-zero cost is not worth two sort
  tuples kept in step by hand."
